Z||000000||Fieroguy||07-10-99||05:52 AM||||I notice when sitting idling that the oil<br>pressure needle sits really close to the<br>red.Is this ok or normal? The pressure<br>goes up fine when driving.Currently<br>running 10/30 synthetic.Car is<br>1986 v6 se auto.
Z||000001||PaUL||07-10-99||07:55 AM||||Thats strange, at idle my gauge reads 80psi, when moving, it drops to about 40psi. Its the opposite of what you just described. At idle oil pressure is high and low when moving.
Z||000002||Patrick||07-10-99||01:38 PM||||<br>Paul, that doesn't sound right at all.<p>It almost sounds as though your oil pump pickup is not able to properly access the "pooled" oil at the bottom of the oil pan. It would be more of a problem when the RPMs are high because more of the oil would be in circulation and <b>not</b> pooled at the bottom of the engine. The pickup would be sucking a quantity of air in along with the oil. When the engine is idling, the oil level would be higher in the pan, and the pickup would have an easier time accessing (only) the oil, thus increasing the oil pressure.<p>This is all conjecture, but I'd be interested to hear if anyone agrees with me.<p>
Z||000003||lowCG||07-10-99||03:43 PM||||I've seen this before,it's a situation where the crankshaft thrust clearance is a bit loose,nothing to worry about.Might be a good idea to check the rod bearings though,cheap insurance.
Z||000004||GREENDANO||07-11-99||01:43 PM||||Best bet if you just want to freshen up the motor is to install a new set of stock main and rod bearings with a new oil pump.  I was going to do that also, but I decided after 150,000 miles of hard wear, from the original owner, was enough and I built a 3.2 instead.<br>   Dan-O
Z||000005||theogre||07-11-99||06:13 PM||||If oil pressure is really that low.... you have some sort of a problem..... bearing wear, pump wear, any of it, could cause that. If need be hook up a second oil pressure gauge to test it and make sure it's not a flaky gauge/sender.<p>As for circulation,,,,,  The volume of the system is nearly constant. There is a bit more retention in the valve rocker area at higher RPM, but not enough to lower the pan volume to the point where the pump sucks air. There are only 3 things that make a pump suck air. 1. low oil level. 2. windage/G force keeping the oil away. 3. bad pickup tube.<p>
Z||000006||DJRice||07-12-99||07:18 AM||||You guys have me wondering about my oil pressure now.  Mine use to stay at about 80psi, but then the pressure sender started to leak and I replaced it. Now in the morining the oil pressure is about 80 at startup and while crusing, but at Idle it drops to 40-50 psi.  The pressure never gets really close to the red zone but the 2.8 has 137k. Should I be worried?  <p>Maybe I just need to change the type of oil i use?  I still use the recommended 5w30.
Z||000007||batboy||07-12-99||10:42 AM||||When the crank bearings and oil pump start getting a little wear (like with high mileage cars), the oil pressure might drop a bit when the engine is warm and at idle. If you have 20-25 psi at idle and 35-40 psi when driving it, that should be ok for normal driving. A tight or fresh engine usually won't show much oil pressure change with rpm and typically runs at a high pressure than an old tired motor. I think 10W30 works good in the summer and 5W30 is great for winter, except interior Alaska maybe.
Z||000008||theogre||07-12-99||08:14 PM||||As long as the pressure is up 40-50 PSI you shouldn't have a problem.<p>Try just changing the oil and filter. It's likely due anyway, and that will eliminate the posibility a flakey filter, or shot oil.
Z||000009||Yellow85||07-13-99||02:09 AM||||I was wonderling what is gage right under rpm meter?(on 85 GT).<br>My oil get bad so fast(whithin 1500km to 2500km). I hardly use above 4000rpm now.  <br>I tried few kinds of oil(5w-30 or 10w-30), but i don`t see any different. Is this telling me my car havig engine problems?
Z||000010||theogre||07-13-99||02:10 PM||||It could be. If you have allot of blow by the oil gets crappy pretty fast.
